<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajgad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajgad is a hill fort in the Sahyadri range near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. It was one of the key forts secured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> in building his power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. It served as his capital for many years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a>, and it was central to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajgad.jpg" alt="Rajgad" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohan sangodkar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suvela_machi,Rajgad_fort,_Maharashtra,India_July2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rajgad, whose name means ruling fort, stands in the Sahyadri range about 60 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and about 15 km west of Nasrapur, 1,376 m above sea level. It was built on a hill called Murumbadevi Dongar, the mountain of the goddess Murumba, and the fort itself was formerly known as Murumbdev. The base of the hill is about 40 km across, which made the fort hard to lay siege to and added to its strategic value.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Treasure found at the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-fort">fort of Torna</a> paid for building and fortifying Rajgad, which then served as the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> for almost 26 years, until the capital was moved to Raigad Fort. Shivaji is held to have spent more days at Rajgad than at any other fort, and many events of his life took place there, among them the birth of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaram-i">Rajaram I</a>, the death of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saibai">Saibai</a> and his return from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>.</p><p>The head of Afzal Khan was buried in the walls of the Mahadarwaja of the Balle Killa. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> signed the Treaty of Purandar with the Mughal general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-i">Jai Singh I</a> in 1665, handing 23 forts over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, Rajgad was one of the 12 forts he kept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins of the fort include palaces, water cisterns and caves, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a> temple on the fort can hold around 50 people. In 2025 Rajgad was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List as a component site of the Maratha Military Landscapes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is much visited, above all during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, and is large enough that visitors often stay overnight.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajgad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The founder, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj (1630–1680), son of the Bijapur commander Shahaji Bhonsle, used the rugged terrain of the Western Ghats and his grasp of guerrilla warfare to seize strategically placed forts such as Torna, <b>Rajgad</b> and later Raigad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pant Sachivs ruled a hilly territory of forts and small farms along the Western Ghats, including Rohida and <b>Rajgad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhor-state">Bhor State</a></li></ul></section>
